WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.105
Welcome to Sprintlab.

00:00:02.405 --> 00:00:04.236
Tutorials teach you to code.

00:00:04.536 --> 00:00:08.935
Sprintlab lets you practise the
part they skip: working in a team.

00:00:09.635 --> 00:00:14.717
For one week you join Workkit, a fictional
company that leases laptops and phones.

00:00:15.018 --> 00:00:17.200
Your colleagues are AI: a team lead,

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:20.230
a project manager, two
developers and a customer.

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:25.172
You sign in with GitHub and create
your own copy of the Workkit code.

00:00:25.472 --> 00:00:28.855
You work on it on your own
computer, with real Git.

00:00:29.555 --> 00:00:31.342
Each day brings new tickets.

00:00:31.642 --> 00:00:34.955
You pick one, create a branch
and open a pull request.

00:00:35.255 --> 00:00:37.405
The board follows along by itself.

00:00:38.105 --> 00:00:42.042
Within a minute or two, a colleague
reviews your code on GitHub.

00:00:42.342 --> 00:00:45.667
You fix what they ask, and
merge when it is approved.

00:00:46.367 --> 00:00:49.015
Meanwhile, the sprint
does what real sprints do.

00:00:49.315 --> 00:00:52.205
Requirements change, a
customer reports a problem,

00:00:52.205 --> 00:00:54.921
and a colleague asks you
to review their work.

00:00:55.621 --> 00:00:57.167
Stuck? Ask in the chat.

00:00:57.467 --> 00:01:00.252
Colleagues give you a
hint, not the answer.

00:01:00.952 --> 00:01:05.788
At the end, your team lead evaluates your
sprint and shows you what to work on next.

00:01:06.488 --> 00:01:08.168
Start with the two-day demo.

00:01:08.468 --> 00:01:10.777
Sign in with GitHub and meet your team.

